import SAP.P001d.Defs
import SAP.P001d.Defs.WholeW1
import SAP.P001d.Defs.WholeW2

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

open MeasureTheory

/-- P001-T68; p. 163 (PDF p. 181), Example 4.3.5.
The usual measure-theoretic conditions are assumed; g is measurable.
For independent integrable mean-zero noise and an integrable signal g(X),
the outcome Y = g(X) plus the noise is integrable and has g as a conditional
mean version: E[Y given X = x] = g(x). Equivalently, its conditional expectation
given the sigma algebra generated by X equals g(X) almost surely. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T68 : SAP.P001d.T68WholeStatement := by sorry

/-- P001-T69; pp. 166–167 (PDF pp. 184–185).
The usual measure-theoretic conditions are assumed.
For independent U(0,1) covariates and the specified piecewise CEF, its first
partial derivative is -1 on the branch of probability 4/5 and 9 on the branch
of probability 1/5. The covariate means are (1/2,1/2), APD_1 is
(-1)(4/5) + 9(1/5) = 1, and PDA_1 is -1, so they differ. For any nonempty
sample and fitted CEF with the coordinate derivatives used by the formula,
the empirical-law APD plug-in is the average of those derivatives. Its index
is the coordinate k, with no individual-observation subscript. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T69 : SAP.P001d.T69WholeStatement := by sorry

/-- Definition 4.3.9, pp. 168–169 (PDF pp. 186–187).
For every nonempty sample the printed unsquared polynomial criterion has its affine
expansion, tends to minus infinity as the intercept grows, and has no minimizer.
The corrected criterion is the OLS sum of squared residuals. Invertibility of the
polynomial Gram matrix requires at least J+1 distinct observed covariate values and
gives the unique inverse-formula minimizer. These assertions hold at every finite
degree, hence also at the degrees of any sieve sequence with the printed rates. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T70 : SAP.P001d.T70WholeStatement := by sorry

/-- P001-T71; pp. 169–170 (PDF pp. 187–188).
The slope-only penalty is a seminorm vanishing on the unit intercept, zero penalty gives exactly the OLS minimizer set, and nonnegative penalty gives a convex criterion. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T71 : SAP.P001d.T71Statement := by sorry

/-- P001-T72; Definition 5.1.4: pp. 180–181 (PDF pp. 198–199). Theorem 5.1.5: p. 181 (PDF p. 199).
The positive-scale normal kernel is an integrable probability density with mean x dot beta; the totalized zero-scale formula has mass zero and is not a probability density.
Restriction: Only positive-scale normal-density normalization and its fixed-covariate first moment, together with the totalized zero-scale diagnostic; the implication from the classical conditional-normal model for arbitrary random X,Y to its linear CEF is omitted. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T72 : SAP.P001d.T72Statement := by sorry

/-- Section 5.1.1, p. 181 (PDF p. 199); Theorem 2.2.19, p. 74 (PDF p. 92).
The printed claim is false: on the four equiprobable atoms (X,Y)=(0,1),(0,3),
(1,0),(1,6), the CEF is 2+X and its residuals are -1,1,-3,3, each with mass 1/4.
Their conditional and unconditional means vanish; their covariance with every g(X)
vanishes. The conditional outcome and residual variances agree and are 1 and 9;
the residual variance and the mean conditional outcome variance are both 5.
Nevertheless the displayed joint probability is 1/2 and the product is 1/4,
so CEF residuals need not be independent of X, even for a linear CEF. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T73 : SAP.P001d.T73WholeStatement := by sorry

/-- P001-T74; p. 181 (PDF p. 199); Theorem 2.2.19, pp. 74–75
(PDF pp. 92–93).
The usual measure-theoretic conditions are assumed; functions of X are
measurable functions. For an integrable response and a chosen conditional mean,
the residual has conditional and unconditional mean zero and is uncorrelated
with every measurable function of X whenever the means and residual product
moment exist. If the residual second moment exists, its conditional variance
equals that of Y, and its variance is the expectation of that conditional
variance; a conditional-variance version exists. Residuals need not be
independent of X, as the four-point example shows. In the classical conditional
normal model at positive scale, the specified residual conditional law is
N(0,sigma^2) at every covariate value; the residual has that marginal law and
is independent of X. Pointwise conditional laws refer to the specified model
version, and conditional-expectation identities are almost-sure identities. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T74 : SAP.P001d.T74WholeStatement := by sorry

/-- P001-T75; Definition 5.1.7: pp. 182–183 (PDF pp. 200–201).
Theorem 5.1.8: p. 183 (PDF p. 201).
For fixed x and beta and h valued in [0,1], the two-point mean
0*(1-h(x dot beta)) + 1*h(x dot beta) equals h(x dot beta) and lies in [0,1].
Restriction: Only the fixed-covariate mean calculation and [0,1] bound for the explicit two-point weights 1-h(x dot beta) and h(x dot beta). The conditional-law-to-CEF implication for an arbitrary binary response is omitted. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T75 : SAP.P001d.T75Statement := by sorry

/-- P001-T76; pp. 190–191 (PDF pp. 208–209).
The usual measure-theoretic conditions are assumed.
At every fixed positive scale, the Gaussian likelihood is a positive product of
the displayed normal densities; its logarithm is the displayed sum and the
closed-form normal log-likelihood. Likelihood maximizers, log-likelihood
maximizers and residual-sum-of-squares minimizers in R^(K+1) coincide, so their
sets do not depend on the positive scale. If the Gram matrix is invertible,
the maximizer exists uniquely and equals (X^T X)^(-1) X^T Y. At positive sample
sizes, minimizing the sum is equivalent to minimizing the OLS mean square. -/
theorem SAP.P001d.T76 : SAP.P001d.T76WholeStatement := by sorry
